<% content_for :title, "Buddhism | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="spirituality-buddhism-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="spirituality-buddhism-hero">

    <div class="spirituality-buddhism-hero-content">

      <p class="spirituality-buddhism-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / SPIRITUALITY / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>YOU DON'T</span>
        <span>HAVE TO</span>
        <span>ESCAPE THIS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="spirituality-buddhism-intro">
        SUFFERING. CRAVING. IMPERMANENCE.<br>
        AND LEARNING HOW TO BE HERE ANYWAY.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CONTENT -->
  <section class="spirituality-buddhism-content">

    <!-- INTRO -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-black">

      <p class="sb-number">01 / THE BASIC IDEA</p>

      <h2>
        LIFE IS<br>
        FUCKING HARD.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Buddhism doesn't begin by pretending life is always peaceful,
          beautiful, or fair.
        </p>

        <p>
          It begins with suffering.
        </p>

        <p>
          We experience pain, loss, disappointment, fear, illness,
          frustration, change, craving, and the fact that nothing stays
          exactly how we want it to stay.
        </p>

        <p>
          The point isn't to become someone who never suffers.
          It's to understand suffering differently — and stop adding
          unnecessary suffering on top of the pain that's already here.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-pink">

      <p class="sb-number">02 / THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS</p>

      <h2>
        START WITH<br>
        WHAT HURTS.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          At the center of Buddhism are the Four Noble Truths.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>SUFFERING EXISTS.</h3>
            <p>
              Life includes dissatisfaction, pain, loss,
              uncertainty, and things not going our fucking way.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>SUFFERING HAS CAUSES.</h3>
            <p>
              Craving, clinging, resistance, and wanting reality
              to be different from what it is can intensify suffering.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>SUFFERING CAN CHANGE.</h3>
            <p>
              We don't have to remain trapped in the same relationship
              with craving and suffering forever.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>THERE IS A PATH.</h3>
            <p>
              Buddhism offers a practice for living with greater
              wisdom, awareness, compassion, and freedom.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- CRAVING -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-yellow">

      <p class="sb-number">03 / CRAVING</p>

      <h2>
        I WANT.<br>
        I NEED.<br>
        MAKE THIS STOP.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Craving isn't limited to substances.
        </p>

        <p>
          We crave pleasure. Relief. Control. Certainty.
          Approval. Distraction. More of what feels good.
          Less of what hurts.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the craving isn't even:
          <strong>"I want this."</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          It's:
          <strong>"I don't want to feel what I'm feeling right now."</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Buddhism asks us to notice craving without immediately
          obeying it.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          THE URGE CAN BE HERE<br>
          WITHOUT RUNNING THE FUCKING SHOW.
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- IMPERMANENCE -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-purple">

      <p class="sb-number">04 / IMPERMANENCE</p>

      <h2>
        THIS WILL<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        CHANGE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          One of Buddhism's central teachings is impermanence:
          everything conditioned changes.
        </p>

        <p>
          Feelings change. Thoughts change. Bodies change.
          Relationships change. Circumstances change.
          Cravings change.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's uncomfortable when we desperately want something
          good to stay.
        </p>

        <p>
          But it's also incredibly useful when something hurts.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          THIS MOMENT IS REAL.<br>
          IT IS NOT PERMANENT.
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- MINDFULNESS -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-black">

      <p class="sb-number">05 / MINDFULNESS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IS<br>
        HAPPENING<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Mindfulness isn't making your mind completely blank.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's paying attention to what is happening while it's
          happening — without immediately getting dragged away
          by every thought, feeling, judgment, or urge.
        </p>

        <p>
          Instead of:
        </p>

        <div class="sb-thought">
          "I FEEL LIKE SHIT. I NEED TO MAKE THIS STOP."
        </div>

        <p>
          You might notice:
        </p>

        <div class="sb-thought">
          "I'M HURTING. I'M HAVING AN URGE TO ESCAPE.
          LET ME NOTICE WHAT THIS ACTUALLY FEELS LIKE."
        </div>

        <p>
          That tiny bit of space matters.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- ATTACHMENT -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-pink">

      <p class="sb-number">06 / ATTACHMENT</p>

      <h2>
        LET GO<br>
        DOESN'T MEAN<br>
        DON'T CARE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Non-attachment gets misunderstood as emotional detachment.
        </p>

        <p>
          It doesn't mean you can't love people, enjoy things,
          have goals, or give a shit about your life.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's about noticing when appreciation becomes clinging:
        </p>

        <div class="sb-stack">
          <span>I NEED THIS.</span>
          <span>THIS CAN'T CHANGE.</span>
          <span>THIS PERSON CAN'T LEAVE.</span>
          <span>THIS FEELING HAS TO STAY.</span>
          <span>MY LIFE HAS TO LOOK THIS WAY.</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          You can care deeply without demanding permanence from
          things that were never permanent to begin with.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- COMPASSION -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-yellow">

      <p class="sb-number">07 / COMPASSION</p>

      <h2>
        STOP BEATING<br>
        THE SHIT<br>
        OUT OF YOURSELF.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Compassion is not pretending harmful behavior was okay.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's recognizing suffering without adding hatred to it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can take responsibility for something and still ask:
        </p>

        <div class="sb-stack">
          <span>WHAT WAS I SUFFERING FROM?</span>
          <span>WHAT DID I NEED?</span>
          <span>WHO DID MY ACTIONS HURT?</span>
          <span>WHAT CAN I REPAIR?</span>
          <span>WHAT WOULD A WISER RESPONSE LOOK LIKE NEXT TIME?</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          Accountability and compassion aren't enemies.
          Sometimes compassion is what makes honest accountability possible.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- MIDDLE WAY -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-purple">

      <p class="sb-number">08 / THE MIDDLE WAY</p>

      <h2>
        MAYBE NOT<br>
        EVERYTHING<br>
        HAS TO BE EXTREME.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Buddhism rejects both mindless indulgence and unnecessary
          self-punishment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Middle Way isn't boring moderation for the sake of moderation.
          It's refusing to mistake extremes for freedom.
        </p>

        <p>
          More punishment isn't always discipline.
          More pleasure isn't always happiness.
          More intensity isn't always meaning.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          WHAT ACTUALLY REDUCES SUFFERING<br>
          WITHOUT CREATING MORE OF IT?
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- PRACTICE -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-black">

      <p class="sb-number">09 / PRACTICE</p>

      <h2>
        OKAY.<br>
        SO WHAT DO<br>
        I FUCKING DO?
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          You don't have to become a monk or meditate on a mountain
          for six hours.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start smaller.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-practice-grid">

          <div>
            <span>01</span>
            <p>Notice one urge before acting on it.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>02</span>
            <p>Take three conscious breaths.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>03</span>
            <p>Name what you're feeling without judging it.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>04</span>
            <p>Notice when you're demanding that reality be different.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>05</span>
            <p>Ask what response creates less suffering.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>06</span>
            <p>Practice compassion without abandoning accountability.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- ENDING -->
    <article class="sb-ending">

      <p>
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO SOLVE<br>
        YOUR ENTIRE FUCKING LIFE<br>
        IN THIS MOMENT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        BE HERE.<br>
        NOTICE.<br>
        CHOOSE.
      </h2>

      <p class="sb-ending-copy">
        Then do it again when the next moment shows up.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO SPIRITUALITY",
          spirituality_path,
          class: "sb-back-link" %>

    </article>

  </section>

</main>